---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="For trainers — Scenario Practice"
  description="Read a trainee's scorecard and interpret the review rubric."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>For trainers</h1>

    <h2>Running a cohort</h2>
    <p>
      Send trainees to the scenario page. Pressing <strong>Start this scenario</strong>
      creates their own copy of the repository — nothing to provision and nobody
      to wait for. Ask them to share the repository link with you when they are
      done.
    </p>
    <p>
      Private or public is up to them; the Grade workflow runs either way. If a
      cohort is working the same scenario at once, private repos keep one
      trainee's solution out of another's search results.
    </p>

    <h2>What gets scored automatically</h2>
    <p>
      Every push runs the scenario's test suite in slices — one per work item,
      plus a regression slice covering behaviour that already worked. The
      <strong>Grade</strong> workflow writes a scorecard to its run summary, so a
      trainee opens the latest run to see exactly which items scored. Regression
      carries real weight by design: shipping a broken change should cost more
      than shipping less.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because each repository belongs to the trainee, scores are visible to them
      and to anyone they share the repository with. There is no cohort dashboard.
    </p>

    <h2>Grading integrity</h2>
    <p>
      The numeric score comes from the tests. Reading the diff against the rubric
      is yours to do — automated review is not currently part of the loop. Where a
      judgement call and the tests disagree, the tests are the record.
    </p>

    <h2>The rubric</h2>
    <p>
      Tests cannot see whether work fits the codebase it lives in. Each scenario
      ships a five-item rubric in <code>docs/rubric.md</code> covering where
      business logic sits, whether DTO boundaries hold, whether new endpoints
      validate their input, whether the trainee added tests of their own, and
      whether the diff is free of leftovers. The item IDs are stable, so verdicts
      stay comparable between reviewers and across cohorts.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a scenario</h2>
    <p>
      Scenarios are markdown files in this site's repository. Add one file with the
      required frontmatter, set <code>published</code> to true, and open a pull
      request — the build validates the shape and fails loudly on anything
      missing.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
